| Feature           | Additive          | Multiplicative             |
| ----------------- | ----------------- | -------------------------- |
| Formula           | Y = T + S + C + E | Y = T × S × C × E          |
| Seasonal Effect   | Constant          | Changes with trend         |
| Variation         | Same size         | Increasing/Decreasing size |
| Example           | Temperature       | Sales Revenue              |
| Easy to Interpret | Yes               | Moderate                   |

============

1. Additive Time Series Model

In an additive model, all components are added together.

Y=T+S+C+E

Where:

Y = Actual Time Series Data
T = Trend
S = Seasonality
C = Cyclical Component
E = Error (Random Noise)

Y=T+S+C+E

Meaning

The seasonal effect remains approximately constant over time.

Example: Ice Cream Sales

| Month | Trend | Seasonal Effect | Sales |
| ----- | ----- | --------------- | ----- |
| Jan   | 100   | +20             | 120   |
| Feb   | 110   | +20             | 130   |
| Mar   | 120   | +20             | 140   |

Notice:

Seasonal increase = 20 units every month
Does not change with trend

✅ Use Additive Model when seasonal fluctuations are constant

=========

2. Multiplicative Time Series Model

In a multiplicative model, components are multiplied.

Y=T×S×C×E

Y=T×S×C×E

Meaning

The seasonal effect changes according to the trend.

Example: Online Shopping Sales

| Month | Trend | Seasonal Factor | Sales |
| ----- | ----- | --------------- | ----- |
| Jan   | 100   | 1.2             | 120   |
| Feb   | 200   | 1.2             | 240   |
| Mar   | 300   | 1.2             | 360   |

Notice:

Seasonal effect grows as trend grows.
20% increase on 100 = 20
20% increase on 300 = 60

✅ Use Multiplicative Model when seasonal fluctuations increase or decrease with the level of data.

============

Components Shown in the Image
1. Trend (T)

Long-term direction of data.

Example:

Company revenue increasing every year.
Population growth.

📈 Upward trend:

100 → 120 → 140 → 160
2. Seasonality (S)

Pattern repeating at fixed intervals.

Examples:

Ice cream sales higher in summer.
E-commerce sales during Diwali.
Hotel bookings during holidays.

🔄 Repeats every year/month/week.

3. Cyclical Component (C)

Long-term ups and downs that do not occur at fixed intervals.

Examples:

Economic boom and recession.
Stock market cycles.

📉📈 Cycle length is not fixed.

4. Error / Noise (E)

Random variation that cannot be predicted.

Examples:

Sudden strike
Natural disaster
Unexpected event

========

When do we use Additive Time Series?

👉 When seasonal variations are roughly constant over time.

When do we use Multiplicative Time Series?

👉 When seasonal variations increase or decrease proportionally with the trend.

